      "post": {
        "id": 418,
        "slug": "reduce-no-shows",
        "link": "https://www.example.com/blog/reduce-no-shows/",
        "status": "publish",
        "date": "2025-03-14T09:00:00",
        "modified": "2026-06-02T11:20:00",
        "author": 7,
        "title": {
          "rendered": "How to reduce no-shows: 9 tactics that work"
        },
        "content": {
          "raw": "<p>Every business owner knows the feeling. You block out an hour, get the room ready, and wait. In this post we share a few ideas we have picked up over the years.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"https://www.example.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/empty-chair-clock.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"1200\" height=\"630\"><figcaption>An empty chair at 10:15 on a Tuesday.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>1. Send reminders</h2>\n<p>Reminders are the cheapest fix on this list. Most clients who miss a visit simply forgot, and a message the day before brings the booking back to mind. If you want to reduce no-shows with one change this week, start here.</p>\n<p>Our <a href=\"https://www.example.com/features/reminders/\">automatic reminders</a> go out by text and email, and you choose the timing.</p>\n<h4>Text or email?</h4>\n<p>Text reminders get read within minutes, while email often sits unopened. Send text reminders for next-day visits and keep email for bookings made weeks ahead. Clients can reply to text reminders to confirm or cancel.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"https://www.example.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/reminder-settings.png\" alt=\"reminder-settings.png\" width=\"900\" height=\"560\"><figcaption>Set the reminder to go out 24 hours before the visit.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>2. Take deposits</h2>\n<p>Ask for a deposit when the client books, and say in plain words when it is kept. A deposit is the strongest way to reduce no-shows for long or expensive services, because the client has something to lose.</p>\n<figure><img src=\"https://www.example.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/deposit-settings.png\" alt=\"Deposit settings screen in Example Booking Software\" width=\"900\" height=\"560\"></figure>\n<h2>3. Keep a waitlist</h2>\n<p>A waitlist will not reduce no-shows on its own, but it fills the gap when one happens. When a slot opens, the next person on the list gets a text and can claim it.</p>\n<h2>4. Let clients reschedule online</h2>\n<p>Many no-shows are people who could not face a phone call to move their booking. A reschedule link in every reminder lets them pick a new time in a few taps, which helps reduce no-shows without extra work at the front desk.</p>\n<h2>5. Confirm by text</h2>\n<p>Ask clients to reply YES to confirm. Anyone who has not replied by the evening before gets a call. Confirmation requests reduce no-shows because they turn a passive booking into a small promise.</p>\n<h2>6. Overbook carefully</h2>\n<p>If a slot type is missed often, book one extra client into it. Overbooking does not reduce no-shows, it only softens them, so use it for short services where a double booking can be absorbed.</p>\n<h2>7. Follow up with repeat no-shows</h2>\n<p>A client who misses twice gets a friendly message and a request to pay a deposit next time. Most businesses that track this find a few clients cause most of the missed visits, and handling them directly is the fastest way to reduce no-shows.</p>\n<h2>8. Thank clients who show up</h2>\n<p>A short thank-you after each visit, with a link to book again, keeps the relationship warm. Clients who feel noticed are less likely to skip.</p>\n<h2>9. Make cancelling easy</h2>\n<p>A client who can cancel in one tap will cancel instead of disappearing. Put a cancel link in every reminder and accept cancellations up to the last hour.</p>\n<h2>Wrap-up</h2>\n<p>No single tactic will reduce no-shows to zero. Start with appointment reminders and deposits, then add the rest as your calendar fills. Our <a href=\"https://www.example.com/pricing/\">plans</a> include reminders on every tier.</p>"
        },
        "excerpt": {
          "raw": ""
        },
